c728d55  tests/planner/scope.test.mjs:42  WARNING  The test relies on `discoverTasks` returning tasks in an order that, when sorted by `compareTaskIds`, results in `['SP-999', 'SP-1000']`. However, `discoverTasks` uses `fs.readdirSync`, which returns entries in filesystem-dependent order (often lexicographic). If the filesystem returns `SP-1000-test` before `SP-999-test`, the input to `parseScope` will be unsorted. The test will only pass if `compareTaskIds` correctly implements numerical sorting (e.g., extracting the numeric part). If `compareTaskIds` is lexicographic (e.g., `a.localeCompare(b)`), `SP-1000` will sort before `SP-999` because '1' < '9', causing the test to fail. The test is fragile because it depends on the implementation of `compareTaskIds` which is not shown, and the input order from `readdirSync` is not guaranteed to be sorted. A safer test would mock `discoverTasks` or explicitly sort the input.
aee6cc5  tests/planner/scope.test.mjs:88  WARNING  The test relies on `discoverTasks` returning task IDs in a specific order (SP-1000 before SP-999) to verify that `parseScope` sorts them numerically. However, `discoverTasks` uses `fs.readdirSync`, which returns entries in filesystem-dependent order (often alphabetical). On many systems, `SP-1000-test` will be read before `SP-999-test` because '1' < '9'. If the filesystem returns `SP-999-test` first, the input to `parseScope` is already sorted, and the test will pass even if `compareTaskIds` is broken (e.g., lexicographic). To make the test robust and actually verify the sorting logic, the test should mock `discoverTasks` or explicitly construct the `discoveredTasks` array in a known unsorted order (e.g., SP-1000 first) rather than relying on filesystem enumeration order.
